//! Interrupt: an IRQ line bound to a waitable, and the whole of a userspace
//! driver's kernel surface (spec §2, §9).
//!
//! Two operations wide: attach it to a Waiter and park, then `ack` when the
//! device has been serviced. The attach is the Waiter's, in `sos.waiter`; the
//! ack is here, with the object it ends a fire on.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, InterruptHandle, NO_HANDLE}

import sos.floor.{checked, sos_handle_release, sos_interrupt_ack, handle_rights}

/// A handle to an Interrupt, an IRQ line bound to a waitable.
///
/// The whole of a userspace driver's kernel surface, two operations wide:
/// attach it to a Waiter and park, then `ack` when the device has been
/// serviced. Everything between those (reading the device's registers, deciding
/// what the interrupt meant, writing a reply) happens in ordinary process code
/// through the MMIO window the image was granted. There is no kernel driver.
///
/// Spec §9's cycle, from the holder's side:
///
///   1. the line fires; the kernel masks it and marks this object ready;
///   2. a `Waiter.wait` answers with this attachment's key and the line;
///   3. the driver touches its device; every register read or write belongs
///      here, before the ack (§9's release rule);
///   4. `ack` unmasks, and a device still asserting fires again immediately.
///
/// At most one unacked fire exists, because step 1 masks. That is what makes a
/// single-task driver non-reentrant with no lock: the line cannot come back
/// while the handler runs.
///
/// Built by `System.interrupt_bind`, handed back by a refused
/// `Process.give(interrupt:)`, and delivered by a message as
/// `PipeHandle.Interrupt` or a boot record through
/// `BootHandle.take_interrupt`: no word constructor, no word extractor.
///
/// It has one name, this wrapper's handle. There is no `mint`, and a Waiter
/// borrows it (`Waiter.add`) and never consumes it, so the holder is always the
/// one that can `ack`. `NoCopy`, and dropping it releases the handle, which
/// destroys the Interrupt: the line is masked and unbound, and bindable again
/// at once. A Waiter attachment does not keep it alive. If one still stands, it
/// reports the destruction as a wait record whose line is the board's no-line
/// value, and keeps reporting it until it is removed (§2.10).
public struct Interrupt {
    public(package) handle: InterruptHandle,
}

extension Interrupt: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Interrupt {
    /// This handle's rights word, read from the kernel (`INFO_OP`), to test
    /// against `InterruptRight` bits. It is the handle's own word, so a sibling
    /// from `mint(rights:)` or a handle received with a keep mask reports the
    /// narrowed set. No right gates it, and it answers for a handle to an
    /// invalid object too, since it describes the handle and not the object. A
    /// live handle resolves, so the error channel carries only a kernel skew
    /// (`SosStatus.Unknown`).
    public func rights(&self) -> Result<UInt32, SosStatus> {
        handle_rights(self.handle)
    }

    /// End this fire and let the device interrupt again (spec §9's ack).
    /// Requires `InterruptRight.Ack`.
    ///
    /// Ack is a release: everything that touches device registers happens
    /// before it, and code after it runs on data already extracted. A driver
    /// that needs register work after the ack serializes it itself; the kernel
    /// does not enforce single-servicing.
    ///
    /// Acking an Interrupt that has not fired terminates the process. The
    /// caller can check (the only way to learn about a fire is to be told about
    /// one), and tolerating it would let the next fire be acked by a driver
    /// that never serviced it.
    public func ack(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_interrupt_ack(self.handle))
    }
}
